  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q30-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "30",
    "prompt": "In a two-digit number, the units digit is three times the tens digit.\nWhen 36 is added to the number, the digits swap positions.\nWhat is the original 2-digit number?\n\n(Hint: a number can be written in terms of its digits in this way: $35=3(10)+5$; $\\overline{ab}=10a+b$, where $\\overline{ab}$ denotes the two-digit number.)",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$26$",
      "worked": "Let $a$ be the tens digit and $b$ be the units digit.\n$$\\begin{align*}b&=3a &&(1)\\\\10a+b+36&=10b+a &&(2)\\\\36&=9b-9a\\\\4&=b-a\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}4&=3a-a\\\\4&=2a\\\\a&=2\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $a=2$ into (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}b&=3(2)\\\\b&=6\\end{align*}$$\nThe original number is 26. Check: $26+36=62$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 78
  },
